Subject: BioBlitz partnership request: acoustic biodiversity evidence and student review

Hello Dr. O'Donnell,

My name is Rodrigo Castro, and I am developing Listening.bio, a working platform for auditable acoustic biodiversity monitoring. It accepts environmental WAV recordings, runs BirdNET candidate detection, preserves provenance, supports confirm/reject review, and exports CSV and GeoJSON data.

Macaulay's BioBlitz model appears especially compatible because Listening.bio is not intended to replace naturalists. It organizes machine-generated candidates so students and experts can inspect, correct, and learn from them.

I would like to explore a small collaboration that compares acoustic candidates with BioBlitz observations, creates a student review exercise, or tests whether environmental audio adds useful temporal coverage around a future event. A first pilot could use three to five sites and 50 to 100 recordings, with transparent reporting of correct detections, false candidates, and model limitations.

Listening.bio can provide the complete software workflow, processing, review interface, maps, and research-ready exports. I am seeking scientific and educational feedback, help shaping the validation method, and an appropriate BioBlitz or green-space context.

Would you be open to a 20-minute conversation about whether this could complement a future Macaulay BioBlitz? I can share a live demonstration and one-page protocol beforehand.

